          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.083,
            0.744,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "We observe that the candle that was not covered (Fig. 5.6a) continues to burn, whereas the candle that was covered with a glass tumbler (Fig. 5.6b) stops burning after some time. Why does this happen?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.159,
            0.744,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": "Since the candle covered by the glass tumbler does not have a continuous supply of air, the flame gets extinguished soon. The component of air that supports combustion is oxygen. This can be confirmed by the presence of carbon dioxide in the glass tumbler inverted on the candle. How can you test the presence of carbon dioxide gas?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.272,
            0.744,
            0.365
          ],
          "content": "You can test the carbon dioxide gas by adding a small amount of lime water in the petri dish. You will notice that it turns milky. This carbon dioxide was formed by the carbon from the wax and the oxygen from the air. In other words, oxygen is required for combustion."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.382,
            0.426,
            0.401
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "SCIENCE AND SOCIETY"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.182,
            0.406,
            0.763,
            0.439
          ],
          "content": "If a person’s clothes catch fire, what is the best way to extinguish the fire?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.182,
            0.439,
            0.761,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": "Wrap a blanket or cloth around the person. This cuts off the supply of air, and the fire gets extinguished."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.478,
            0.177,
            0.505
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.182,
            0.476,
            0.763,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": "**Caution** — Synthetic blanket or cloth should never be used to put out a fire, as these can melt and stick to the skin."
        },
        {
